1. e4 b6 2. d4 Bb7 3. Bd3 f5 {[%cal Rb7g2,Yf5e4] Black certainly has an idea behind the move, but he has violated too many opening principles.} 4.
exf5 $1 (4. Qh5+ {Recognizing the idea would already be good, even though it does not work here yet.} g6) (4. Nc3 Nf6 5. Qe2 fxe4 6. Nxe4 e6 {leads to a playable position for Black.}) 4... Bxg2 {White should of course have noticed that g2 is hanging; otherwise you should review "active moves" again.} 5. Qh5+ g6 6. fxg6 {After 4.exf5, you should have been able to calculate up to this position and assess that it looks strong.} Nf6 $2 (6... Bxh1 7.
g7#) (6... Bg7 {is the correct continuation. White wins here as well, but considerable subtleties are required, so this will only be covered in opening theory from Part 40 onward.}) 7. gxh7+ $3 (7. g7+ Nxh5 8. gxh8=Q Bxh1 {This is winning, but only because White has restored material equality and will gain further material due to the attack. If Nh5 could be defended, for example, White would still have a long game ahead.} 9. Qxh7 $18 {[%cal
Rh7h5,Rh7g6]}) 7... Nxh5 8. Bg6# 1-0

