1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 d6 $6 {Although this move is playable (with reservations), it violates the Golden Opening Principle of development. The bishop on f8 is deprived of its best square, c5.} 3. Bc4 Bg4 4. h3 Bxf3 $6 {This also only helps White develop.} 5. Qxf3 Nf6 {If you start with active moves, as you should, you find this.} 6. Qb3 $1 {[%cal Gc4f7,Rb3b7] Doppelangriff.} Nxe4 {Black at least wants to take a pawn.} 7. Bxf7+ (7. Qxb7 {This is also good, but weaker. Other moves, however, bring no benefit.}) 7... Kd7 (7... Ke7
8. Qe6#) 8. Qxb7 {[%cal Yb7e4,Yb7a8] Another double attack.} (8. Qe6+ {If you find a forced win here, you can prefer this move to Qxb7.}
Kc6 9. Qd5+ Kd7 10. Qxe4 $18 {An extra piece. If you see both options, you can, for example, count the material and then decide.}) (8. Be6+ $2 {It is a check, but has no continuation.} Ke7 9. d3 Nf6 {If White now takes on b7, only the bishop on e6 is unnecessarily exposed.}) 8... Ng5 (8... Nc6 {defends against both, but} 9. Bd5 $18 {
[%cal Rd5e4,Rd5c6] erneut Doppelangriff.}) 9. Bd5 $1 {A four-point advantage is better than gaining a two-point advantage.} (9. Qxa8 Nxf7 {Here White has a rook and pawn for a knight. Good, but there is something better.}) 9... Na6 10. Qc6+ {A small subtlety: Black cannot play Be7.} Ke7 11. Qxa8 1-0

