1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 f6 $2 {violates the development principle. It also weakens the e8-h5 diagonal.} 3. Nxe5 $1 (3. d4 $16 {Just for reference: simply following the opening principles also gives White a large advantage.}) 3... fxe5 $2 (
3... Qe7 4. Nf3 Qxe4+ 5. Be2 $16 {White has an enormous lead in development.}) 4.
Qh5+ Ke7 (4... g6 5. Qxe5+ Qe7 6. Qxh8 Qxe4+ 7. Be2 (7. Kd1 $5 {Weakens the king somewhat, but keeps more material.} d5 {[%cal Rc8g4]} 8. f3 $18) 7... Qxg2
8. Rf1 {White has a material advantage.}) 5. Qxe5+ Kf7 6. Bc4+ {Development with tempo, combined with an attack.} Kg6 7. Qf5+ (7. d4 {is strong, but less forcing.}
) 7... Kh6 8. d4+ {Again development and attack with check.} g5 {How can White bring even more pieces into play?} 9. h4 $1 {With one move, h4 brings both the pawn and the rook on h1 into play.} Kg7 10. Qf7+ Kh6 11. hxg5# 1-0

