1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Nf3 (3. d4 $6 {follows the opening principles, but it allows} Qh4+ {after which White stands poorly.} 4. Ke2 {does not lose material, but then d4 has not paid off.} (4. g3 fxg3 5. Nf3 g2+ (5...
Qxe4+ {gives a smaller advantage, but still enough to refute the line.} 6.
Qe2 Qxe2+ 7. Bxe2 gxh2 8. Rxh2 {Three extra pawns in the endgame.}) 6. Nxh4 gxh1=Q {An extra rook.})) 3... g5 {Black spends time defending his pawn. It does not follow the opening principles, but it is a playable option.} (3...
d5 $1 {is not better per se, but is easier for Black to play.} 4. exd5
Nf6 $132 {Do not take with the queen!}) 4. Bc4 {develops a piece, attacks f7, and prepares kingside castling. Then attack before Black can consolidate.} f6 $2 {But this move is now unnecessary.} 5. Nxg5 $3 (5. Nd4 {[%cal Rd1h5] also threatens Qh5+, but Black can defend against it.} h5 ({or} 5... Ne7
6. Qh5+ Ng6)) 5... fxg5 {otherwise Black has simply lost a pawn. In addition, Nf7 was also threatened.} 6. Qh5+ Ke7 7. Qxg5+ (7. Qf7+ Kd6 8. Qd5+ Ke7 9.
Qe5#) 7... Ke8 8. Qh5+ {is the most precise continuation.} (8. Qe5+ $2 {is a weak option.} Qe7 9. Qxh8 Qxe4+ {Black gets some counterplay.} 10. Be2 Qxg2 11.
Rf1 Qg7 12. Qxg7 Bxg7 13. Rxf4 $16 {White is an exchange up, but victory is still a long way off.}) (8. Bf7+ {Deflection and winning the queen.} Kxf7 9. Qxd8 $18)
8... Ke7 9. Qe5# 1-0

