1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d4 exd4 6. cxd4 Bb6 $6 {[%cal Yd4c5,Yd4e5,Ye4d5,Ye4f5] simply gives White the center.} (6... Bb4+ {is correct.}) 7. e5 $1 (7. Qb3 $6 {The attack is far too early.} O-O 8. Ng5 d5) (7. d5
$1 {is also strong.} Nxe4 $5 (7... Ne7 8. e5 $1 {as in the game.}) 8. dxc6
Nxf2 9. Qe2+ $18) (7. Nc3 d6 8. O-O Bg4 $14 {is not so bad for Black. Therefore White must act quickly.}) 7... Ng8 (7... d5 {is generally the better try. White can exceptionally win here as well, but it is harder to find.} 8. exf6 dxc4 9. d5 $1 {Black can play neither Ne7 nor Qe7+.} (9. fxg7 Rg8 10. O-O Nxd4 $11 {is okay for Black.}) 9... Nb4
(9... Na5 10. fxg7 Rg8 11. O-O {The knight on a5 does not participate.}) (9... Nb8 10. fxg7 Rg8
11. O-O Rxg7 12. Bh6 Rg6 13. Re1+ Be6 $5 14. Bf4 $18) 10. Qa4+) 8. d5 $1 {This is a perfect example of a center and what it is meant to achieve.} (8. Ng5 Nh6
) (8. O-O d5) 8... Nce7 9. d6 $1 {Even more pressure. Even if d6 becomes weak, the bishop on c8 and rook on a8 cannot get out at all. Black's king also loses the right to castle.} (9.
O-O d6 {Black gets some breathing room.} 10. e6 $2 {A sample variation from a student.} (10. Nc3 $16 {is still not good, but gives less of an advantage than d6.})
10... fxe6 11. dxe6 Nf6 12. Re1 O-O 13. Nc3 d5 {Suddenly Black is better, and the center merely becomes weak.}) 9... Nc6 (9... Nf5 10. Qd3 {gives White more time.} g6 (10... Ngh6 11. Bg5) 11. Bg5) (9... cxd6 10. exd6 Ng6 11. Qd5 {similar to the game.} Qf6 12. O-O Nh6 13. Re1+ Kf8 14. Qe4 {with a back-rank mate.}) 10. Qd5
$1 {If you can attack, it is better than waiting.} Nh6 11. Bxh6 Rf8 12.
Bxg7 Nb4 13. Qd2 Rg8 14. Bf6 1-0

