1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Qe7 $6 {violates an opening principle, namely bringing the queen out too early.} 5. O-O (5. d4 $2 {dagegen war genau
Qe7 gerichtet} exd4 6. cxd4 Qxe4+) 5... d6 6. d4 {Now the idea with Qe7 is useless.} Bb6 7. Bg5 (7. Ng5 $6 {already violates an opening principle: moving the same piece several times. You can do it if you win something by force, but that is not the case here.} Nh6 8. Qf3 O-O (8... exd4)) (7. Bb5 $6 {also moves the same piece a second time.} Bd7) (7. d5 $6 {is a typical mistake in this position. White does not gain more control over the center, but rather less. It also blocks the bishop on c4.}) (7. Qb3 $2 {is just as bad as Ng5.} exd4 (7... Nf6 8. Ng5 O-O {[%csl Yd4,Ye4]})) (7. Qa4 $6 {wins nothing, and Qa4 is poorly placed after} Bd7) (7. Re1 {is okay as a positional move.}) 7... f6 {Yes, the bishop has to move again, but f6 blocks the knight on g8!} (7... Nf6 {is a pin for White.}) 8. Bh4 (8. Be3 $6 {loses a pawn.} exd4 9. Nxd4 Qxe4 10. Re1 Nge7 {White has compensation, but it is not easy to play.}) (8. Qb3 $2 Na5) (8. Nxe5 $2 {The idea of Qh5 does not work either.} dxe5) 8... g5 9. Nxg5 $3 fxg5 10. Qh5+ {and we return to the initial position. Greco gave four games that reached this position.} Kd7 {Damit gab es 2 Partien} (10... Kd8 11. Bxg5 Nf6 12. Qh6 (
12. Qh4 {also attacks f6, but is weaker than Qh6. The main difference is that the rook on f8 is not attacked.} Rf8) 12... Rf8 13. f4 $1 {[%csl Rd8,Gf1] Brings the rook into play. However, you absolutely have to calculate the variation.} (
13. Bg8 {A bad trap; Black does not have to take on g8.}) (13. dxe5 Nxe5 {
[%cal Re5c4]}) (13. d5 {is bad because it closes the position. That only helps Black.} Nb8 (13... Na5 14. Be2)) 13... exd4 (13... Bd7 14. fxe5
dxe5 15. Rxf6 $1 $18 {[%cal Rg5d8] The bishop is stronger than the rook.} (15. Bxf6 Rxf6
16. Rxf6 {is also winning.})) 14. e5 $1 {White now even sacrifices the entire rook.} (14. Kh1 {would otherwise be necessary to get out of check.}) 14...
dxc3+ (14... dxe5 15. fxe5 Nxe5 16. Rxf6 $1 {[%csl Gg5][%cal Rg5d8]} (16. Bxf6
$2 Rxf6 17. Rxf6 Nxc4 $19 {Black has a material advantage.}) (16. Qxf8+ Qxf8 17.
Rxf6 Qe7 $19) 16... Rxf6 17. Bxf6) 15. Kh1 cxb2 {[%cal Rb2a1] Black gets a new queen, so the game is very close. White is faster, however.} 16.
exf6 bxa1=Q $2 (16... Rxf6 {also loses, but is a better try.} 17.
Bxf6 bxa1=Q 18. Bxa1 {is technically sufficient as well, because White has regained (almost) all the material while the attack remains.} (18. Qf8+ Kd7 19. Bxe7 Nxe7 20.
Re1 {leads to checkmate.}) 18... Bd4 19. Bc3 $1 {threatens Re1 and develops at the same time.} Bxc3 20. Nxc3 $18) 17. fxe7+ Nxe7 18. Qxf8+ Kd7 {White can already simply take on e7 with a winning position, or} 19. Bb5+ $5 Nc6 20. Qe7# {1-0 (20) Greco,
G-NN Europe 1620}) (10... Kf8 {begegnete Greco einmal} 11. f4 $3 {[%csl Gc4,Gf1,Gh4,Gh5] Another example showing that capturing is not automatic and should not be. White brings the rook on f1 into play.} (11. Bxg5 {was his continuation. He won the game, but was actually losing.} Qe8 (
11... Qg7 $17 12. Bxg8 Kxg8) 12. Qf3+ $2 (12. Bh6+ $11) 12... Kg7 13. Bxg8 (13.
Qg3 Qg6 $19) 13... Rxg8 $4 (13... Kxg8 $19 14. Bf6 exd4) (13... Qg6 $1 {as an intermediate move does not even allow Bf6 and still wins the piece.}) 14. Qf6# {
1-0 (14) Greco,G-NN Europe 1620}) 11... exd4 (11... gxh4 12. fxe5+ Kg7 13. Rf7+
Qxf7 14. Qxf7+ Kh6 {White has enough material and still has an attack as well.}
15. Qf8+ Kh5 {[%cal Gc4e2,Gc4f7,Rc4g8,Rf8g7]} 16. Bf7+ (16. Qg7) (16. Bxg8)
16... Kg4 17. Qg7+) (11... exf4 12. Rxf4+ gxf4 13. Bxe7+ $18 Ngxe7 (13... Kxe7
14. Qf7+ Kd8 15. Qf8+ Kd7 16. Qg7+) 14. Qf7#) (11... Nf6 12. fxg5 {The knight on f6 is pinned.}) 12. fxg5+ Kg7 13. Rf7+ Qxf7 14. Qxf7#) 11. Bxg5 Qf8 {Winning here is quite difficult.} (11... Qg7 {One game continued as follows.} 12. Be6+ $3
{is absurd under normal circumstances, but the rule of looking at active moves tells us to examine exactly this.} (12. Bf7 Nf6 $19 (12... Nge7 $2 {Small bonus question: how does White win here?} 13. Qg4+ Kd8 14. Bxe7+ Nxe7 15. Qxg7 $18)) 12... Kxe6 13. Qe8+ Nce7
14. d5#) 12. Bf7 $1 {threatens mate in one. White must not hesitate for too long.} (12. Be2
Nf6) (12. Rd1 Nf6) (12. Bxg8 Rxg8 13. Qxh7+ {White wins a third pawn, but Black then has the active pieces.} Qg7 14. Qxg7+ Rxg7 $19 {Now Black is better.}) (12. f4 exd4 {This is not an automatic win.}) (12. d5 $4 {would be the worst move because it now protects the black king.}) 12... Nce7 (12...
Nge7 {As always, you have to look for active moves, and then you find} 13. Bh6 $1 (13.
Bf6 exd4 $13 {Black gets enough material, so it is unclear.}) 13... Qd8
(13... Qxf7 14. Qxf7) 14. Qg4+ {Black has to give up the piece because Qd8 takes away the king's retreat square.} Nf5 15. Qxf5+ Ke7 16. Bg5+ Kf8 {White still keeps the queen.} 17. Be6+ Kg7 18. Qf7# (18. Bxd8)) (12... d5 {gives the king the d6-square, but costs too much.} 13. exd5 Nce7 14. dxe5 $18) (12... Nd8
{Exercise: find the win.} 13. Qh3+ Kc6 (13... Ne6 14. Bxe6+ Kc6 15. Bd5+ {is the same variation, only with one knight less.}) 14. Bd5+ {or d5+.} Kb5
15. Qd3+ Ka5 16. b4+ Ka4 17. Bb3#) 13. dxe5 $1 {The idea is less about exchanging pawns and more about opening lines toward the king.} (13. Qg4+ {The check achieves nothing; it only helps Black.} Kd8 14. Bxg8 Bxg4) 13... h6 (13... dxe5 {Exercise: find the win.} 14. Rd1+ (14. Qd1+ $5 Kc6 15. Qa4+ {is not a bad idea either.}) 14... Kc6 15. Be8+ Kc5 16. Be3+ Kc4 17. Qe2# (17. Na3#) (17. b3#)) 14. Bh4 (
14. e6+ {is also good as an intermediate move, but Black is not threatening dxe5.})
14... Rh7 15. e6+ Kc6 (15... Kd8 16. e5 $5 {Keep opening the position.} ({or develop.} 16. Nd2 {Black cannot do anything.})) 16. Be8+ Qxe8 17. Qxe8+
{1-0 (17) Greco,G-NN Europe 1620} 1-0

