1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Bc4 {is a variation in which White allows the check on h4 in exchange for gaining more time for development.} Qh4+ 4. Kf1 Bc5 5. d4
$1 {Do not defend with Qf3; active moves come first.} Bb6 6. Nf3 Qe7 7. Bxf4
$5 (7. Nc3 {Morphy played this against his father; it appears later in this series.}) 7... Qxe4 8. Bxf7+ $1 Kf8 (8... Kxf7 9. Ng5+) 9. Bg3 {The pawn structure is symmetrical, but White has a large lead in development.} Nh6
10. Nc3 Qe7 11. Bb3 c6 {Development is almost complete; now it is time to develop a plan. That plan should be an attack, so put the rooks on open files.} 12. Qd3 d5 13. Re1 {Greco also had this game twice in his lifetime.} Qf6 {was the second game. Many moves win here.} (13... Qf7 {was another game by Greco.} 14. Bd6+ Kg8 15. Re7 {All pieces enter the attack with tempo.} Qf6 (15... Qf8 16. Nxd5 $1 {opens the bishop.} cxd5 17. Bxd5+
Nf7 18. Bxf7+ {is enough to win, but is not the only way.} Qxf7 19. Rxf7
Kxf7 {If you cannot find a win now, then} 20. Kf2 {simply play soundly and simplify into the endgame. White has a material advantage.} Nc6 21. Re1) 16.
Nxd5 Qxd6 17. Nf6+ Kf8 18. Re8# {1-0 Greco,G-NN Europe 1620}) 14. Bh4 (14. Nxd5
$1 {Sacrificial possibilities usually arise when all the pieces are ideally placed.} cxd5 15. Bxd5 {[%cal Gd3a3] Black is helpless against Qa3+.}
Nc6 16. Qa3+ Ne7 17. Bh4) (14. Bxd5 $1 cxd5 15. Nxd5 Qd8 16. Qa3+ {also wins.} Kg8 (16... Kf7 17. Ne5+) 17. Ne7+) 14... Qg6 (14... Bf5 {a good move, but too late.} 15. Qe3 Qf7 16. Nxd5 $1 {the simplest way.} cxd5 17. Bxd5 Qd7
18. Qa3+) 15. Be7+ Kg8 16. Qxg6 hxg6 17. Nxd5 $1 cxd5 18. Bxd5+ Kh7 (18... Nf7
19. Ng5) 19. Ng5# {1-0 (19) Greco,G-NN Europe 1620} 1-0

