7. Nc3 {This is the Italian Gambit, associated with Greco himself.} Nxe4
8. O-O {[%cal Rc3e4] White sacrifices the pawn.} (8. Qe2 $2 d5) 8... Nxc3 {The move is natural and leads to incredible variations.} (8... Bxc3 {Today we know that Black has to continue this way.} 9. d5 $1 $44 {with compensation, but no advantage.} (9. bxc3 $2 d5 {with an advantage for Black.} (9... Nxc3 $4 10. Qe1+
))) 9. bxc3 Bxc3 10. Qb3 $3 {The double exclamation mark is not for the strength of the move, but for the courage to sacrifice the rook. I deliberately do not want to spoil the beauty of the game with computer analysis and subtleties; instead, we will look at the variations that were actually played.} (10. Ba3 {also defends the rook on a1, but White then has to explain how to continue after} d6 {continues.} (10... Bxa1
$2 11. Re1+ Ne7 12. Bxe7 Qxe7 13. Rxe7+ Kxe7 14. Qxa1 $18)) (10. Bxf7+ {wins a pawn, but White is already two pawns down.} Kxf7 11. Qb3+ d5 12.
Qxc3 Re8 $44) (10. Ng5 $2 d5) (10. Qe2+ Qe7) 10... Bxa1 (10... Bxd4 {was the second game. Here Black declines the rook, but in return keeps the bishop for the defense and is still three pawns up.} 11. Bxf7+ (11. Re1+
Kf8 {White cannot avoid Bxf7, so White may as well start with it.}) 11...
Kf8 12. Bg5 (12. Ba3+ d6 {The bishop is on the wrong diagonal.}) 12... Bf6 13.
Rae1 {Since White is not a rook down here, Re8 is a real threat.} Ne7 (
13... Bxg5 14. Nxg5 Qxg5 15. Re8#) 14. Bh5 Ng6 (14... g6 15. Bxf6) (14... d5 {would be the most sensible move; I suspect Greco would then have played like this.} 15. Rxe7 $1
{not the only way.} Qxe7 (15... Kxe7 16. Re1+ Kf8 17. Qxd5 $3 Qxd5 18. Re8#
) (15... Bxe7 16. Bxe7+ Qxe7 17. Re1 {White gains a material advantage and the attack remains.}) 16. Re1 Qxe1+ (16... Qd7 17. Re8+ {is already sufficient.}) 17. Nxe1 Bxg5
18. Qxd5 {[%cal Rd5f7,Rd5g5]} g6 19. Qxg5 gxh5 20. Qf6+ Kg8 21. Nf3 {With correct play White wins; I do not want to explain it further here.})
15. Ne5 $3 (15. Nh4 {is also good.}) 15... Nxe5 (15... d5 16. Bxg6 Bxg5 17. Nf7
$18) (15... Bxg5 16. Qf7#) 16. Rxe5 g6 (16... Bxe5 17. Qf7#) (16... d5 17. Rxd5
) 17. Bh6+ Bg7 18. Rf5+ $1 gxf5 19. Qf7# {1-0 (19) Greco, G-NN Europe 1620}) (
10... Ba5 11. Bxf7+ Kf8 12. Bg5 (12. Ba3+ d6 {does not continue.}) 12... Ne7 13.
Rae1 $1 ({or} 13. Bh5 {as in the game.} d5 14. Ne5 Be6 15. Qf3+ Kg8 16.
Bf7+) 13... Bxe1 14. Rxe1 d6 15. Bxe7+ Qxe7 16. Rxe7 Kxe7 $18 {Material advantage for White.} 17. Bg8 $5 {[%cal Rb3f7]}) 11. Bxf7+ Kf8 12. Bg5 {[%eval 2122,0]} (
12. Re1 d6 13. Re8+ {wins the queen, but White is already a rook down.} Qxe8 14. Bxe8 Kxe8 $17 {Advantage Black!}) (12. Ba3+ d6 {The bishop on a3 does not participate.}) 12... Ne7 13. Ne5 $5 {Greco ignores the bishop and plays for mate.} (13.
Rxa1 {also wins, however.} d5 14. Re1 Kxf7 15. Rxe7+ {[%csl Gd8,Ye7,Yg5]} Qxe7
(15... Kg6 16. Ne5+ Kxg5 17. Qg3+) 16. Bxe7 Kxe7 17. Qxd5 $16 {White still has an advantage because of the active pieces and the exposed king.}) (13. Re1 d5 14. Bxe7+
Qxe7 15. Rxe7 Kxe7 16. Bxd5 {is also positionally winning because of piece activity.}) 13... Bxd4 {[%cal Gb3f3]} 14. Bg6 (14. Ng6+ hxg6 15. Bxg6 {Black can either take on g6 with the knight and have enough material, or even escape completely.} d5 (15... Nxg6 16. Bxd8 c6 $132) 16. Qf3+ (16. Re1 Bf6) 16... Bf5
17. Bxf5 Bf6 {Black survives.}) (14. Qf3 {With perfect play White wins here as well, but finding it is not easy.} Bxe5 (14... d5 15. Be6+) 15.
Bd5+ $1 {preventing Black from playing d5 is the "correct" winning method.} (
15. Bc4+ Bf6 16. Bxf6 d5 $1 17. Bxe7+ Kxe7 18. Re1+ Be6 19. Bxd5 Qd6 {and unfortunately, simply exchanging on e6 does not lead to a win.} 20. Rxe6+ (20. Qb3 {Continuing to play for the attack would be correct, but it is impossible to assess this cleanly beforehand.}) 20... Qxe6 21. Bxe6 Kxe6 22. Qxb7 Rhd8 23. h3 Rac8 24.
Qxa7 c5 $11 {White has to be satisfied with a draw.}) 15... Bf6 16. Bxf6 d6 17.
Bxe7+ Kxe7 18. Qf7#) (14. Bh5 {is weaker than Bg6 because the f5-square is not covered.}) 14... d5 15. Qf3+ Bf5 (15... Nf5 16. Bxd8) (15... Kg8 16. Qf7#)
16. Bxf5 (16. Bxe7+ $2 Kxe7 17. Qxf5 hxg6) 16... Bxe5 {Be6+ or Bg6+?} 17.
Be6+ (17. Bg6+ $2 Bf6 18. Bxf6 hxg6 {White has no more than a draw here.}) 17...
Bf6 18. Bxf6 Ke8 19. Bxg7 {Black resigned.} Qd6 20. Qf7+ Kd8 21. Bxh8 {An extra piece, and mate is still threatened.} 1-0

