1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 {This is a risky continuation for Black because it allows the continuation} (3... Bc5 {is the more common move.}) 4. Ng5 $5 {With correct play Black does not lose, but must sacrifice at least one pawn.} d5 5. exd5 Nxd5 $2 (5... Na5 6. Bb5+ c6 7. dxc6 bxc6 $44 {This is what the correct way of playing looks like.}) 6. Nxf7 $1 {A completely sound sacrifice.} Kxf7 7. Qf3+ {If Black wants to keep the knight, the king has to remain in the center.} Ke6 (7... Kg8 $4 8. Bxd5+ Be6 9. Bxe6#) (7... Ke8 8. Bxd5 {An extra pawn for White.}) 8. Nc3 {develops a piece with tempo.} Nce7 $6 (8... Ncb4 {also covers d5, but additionally threatens Nxc2, giving Black some counterplay and breathing room.}) 9.
O-O c6 {Black can now move the king back, if given the chance.} 10. Re1 (
10. d4 $1 {would be even better.}) 10... Bd7 11. d4 Kd6 12. Rxe5 {White has two pawns and the king is not well protected.} (12. dxe5+ {is also good, but only if White continues actively.} Kc7 13. e6 $1 Be8 14. Rd1 {[%cal Rc3d5,Rc1f4] with Nxd5 or Bf4 next.}) 12... Ng6 (12... Nxc3 13. Bf4 $1 (13. Rxe7 $5 {also wins because of the threat Bf4+.} Bxe7 (13... Kxe7 14. Bg5+ Kd6 15.
Qf4#) 14. Bf4#) 13... Kc7 14. Rxe7+ Kc8 15. Rf7 Nd5 16. Bxd5 cxd5 17. Qxd5 {Three extra pawns, and the attack continues.} Bc6 18. Qe6+ Bd7 19. Qc4+ Bc6 20. Rc7+
Qxc7 21. Bxc7 Kxc7 22. d5) 13. Nxd5 {White does not have to move the rook away.} (13.
Qg3 $1 {is also strong and beautiful. White immediately wins a piece.} Nxc3
14. Re6#) 13... Nxe5 (13... cxd5 14. Rxd5+ Kc7 {Question/exercise: how does White win here?} 15. Qg3+ (15. Bb5 {plays for a pin, but you have to calculate the following.} Bd6 16. Rxd6 $1 Kxd6 17. Bf4+ (17. Qg3+ {also works.} Ke6 (17... Ke7
18. Bg5+) 18. Bc4+ Kf5 (18... Kf6 19. Bg5+) 19. Qf3+) 17... Nxf4 (17... Ke6 18.
Re1+ {with at least winning the queen after a discovered check.} Kf7 (18... Kf6 19. Be5+ Kg5
20. Qe3+) 19. Bg5+) 18. Qxf4+ {Black is a rook up, but the king is completely alone.} Ke7 (18... Ke6 19. Re1+ Kd5 20. Qe5#) (18... Kd5 19. Qe5#) 19.
Re1+ {[%cal Ye1e8,Yf4f8,Yf4d6]} Be6 20. Bc4 Qd6 21. Rxe6+ Qxe6 22. Bxe6 Kxe6
23. Qe5+ Kf7 24. Qd5+ {White will have four extra pawns, so this is clearly winning.})
15... Kb6 (15... Kc8 16. Bb5 {There is no Bd6 here at all.} (16. Rc5+ $2 Bxc5)
16... Bxb5 17. Rxd8+ Kxd8 18. Bg5+ $18) 16. Bd2 {[%cal Rd2a5] threatening Ba5 is the simplest way to win.} (16. Rd6+ $2 Bxd6) (16. Bb5 {also wins here.})) 14.
dxe5+ Kc5 (14... Ke6 15. Nc7+ Kxe5 (15... Ke7 16. Qf7#) 16. Qf4#) (14... Kxe5 {Almost everything wins here.} 15. Qf4+ Ke6 16. Nc7+ Ke7 17. Qe5+ Be6 18. Qxe6#) 15.
Qa3+ {was Greco's choice.} (15. b4+ Kxc4 16. Qd3#) 15... Kxc4 16. Qd3+ Kc5
17. b4# 1-0

