1. e4 e5 2. f4 Nf6 3. Nc3 exf4 4. d4 Bb4 5. Bd3 Qe7 6. Qe2 Nc6 {Exercise: calculate the move e4-e5.} 7. e5 $5 (7. Nf3 d6 (7... d5 $5 8. e5 Ne4 9. Bxe4 dxe4
10. Qxe4 Bg4 $44 {gives Black good compensation, for example:} 11. O-O Bxf3 12. Rxf3
Nxd4 $1 13. Qxd4 Bc5) 8. Bxf4 Bg4 $132) 7... Nxd4 $2 {falls into the trap.}
(7... Nd5 {is stronger, but only if you recognize that Nxd4 loses.} 8. Bd2 {White is not worse, however; it is simply equal.} Nxc3 (8... Nxd4 $2 9.
Nxd5 $18) 9. bxc3 Ba5 10. Nf3 $11) 8. exf6 {White is a piece up; both queens are attacked.} Nxe2 (8... Qxe2+ 9. Ngxe2 $18) 9. fxe7 Nxc3 {This was the position that had to be calculated.} (9... Kxe7 10. Ngxe2 $16 {Black does have three pawns for the piece, but White's lead in development gives White the advantage.})
(9... Nxc1 10. Rxc1 Kxe7 11. Nge2 $16) 10. a3 $1 {The intermediate move wins for White.} (10. Bd2 Nd5 $19) 10... Ba5 11. Bd2 {White gets the piece back again and then wins.} 1-0

