1. e4 {Morphy played this game against his father at the age of ten.}
e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Bc4 Qh4+ 4. Kf1 {White loses a pawn and the right to castle, but has gained a lot of time for development.} Bc5 5. d4 $1 (5. Nf3 $4
Qf2#) 5... Bb6 6. Nf3 (6. Nh3) (6. g3 fxg3) 6... Qe7 7. Nc3 {Morphy, who is regarded by chess players as the unofficial first world champion, adds an element of sound positional play to chess.} (7.
Bxf4 Qxe4 8. Bxf7+ {was an earlier game by Greco.}) 7... Nf6 8. Qd3 $1 {follows the Golden Rules and develops the queen!} (8. Bxf4 {is certainly playable, but why give up the nice pawn on e4?} Nxe4 9. Nd5) (8. e5 $1 {is also strong, but was not played.}) (8. Ng5 $2 O-O (8... h6 9. Nxf7)) (
8. Nd5 $2 {an active move, but it achieves nothing and only moves the same piece a second time.}
Nxd5 9. Bxd5 c6 10. Bb3 Qxe4) 8... c6 $2 {prevents a later Nd5, but violates further opening principles.} 9. Bxf4 d5 $2 {drops a pawn.} 10. exd5 {[%cal Ra1e1] Now White also threatens Re1.} O-O 11. d6 $5 {Now the pawn on d6 participates in the game.} (11. Re1 {is of course also good.}) (11. dxc6 $2 Nxc6 {This only develops Black.}) 11... Qd8 12. Re1 {activates the rook on the open file.} Re8 (12... Nbd7 13. Re7 $1 {brings it even further into the attack.} Re8 $4
14. Bxf7+) 13. Ng5 $1 {White attacks Black's weaknesses with all the pieces.} (
13. Rxe8+ Qxe8 {rather helps Black. Why should White exchange?}) (13. Ne5 {[%cal Rc4f7] is weaker.} Be6) (13. Bxf7+ $5 {even works, but is not in Morphy's style.} Kxf7 14. Ng5+ Kf8 (14... Kg8 15. Qc4+ Kh8 {leads to a smothered mate.} (15... Nd5 16. Nxd5 $18) 16. Nf7+ Kg8 17. Nh6+ $5 {even better than Nxd8.} Kh8 18. Qg8+ Nxg8 19. Nf7#) 15. Qc4 Qd7 16. Re7 Rxe7 17. dxe7+
Qxe7 18. Bd6 $3 $18 {White wins with this maneuver, but all of this would have to be seen when playing 13.Bxf7.} Qxd6 19. Qf7#) 13... Rxe1+ (13... Rf8 14. Re7 {does not improve matters.} (14. Nxf7 $5 Rxf7 15. Re7)) (13... Be6 {[%cal Rg5e6] does not work here; that is the advantage of Ng5 over Ne5.}) 14. Kxe1 Qe8+ 15. Kd2 $1 {An exclamation mark for what White actually wants to achieve. The idea is to bring the rook on h1 into play!} (15. Kf1 $6 {The king is safe, but the rook on h1 does not participate. After other moves Black can give checks.}) (15. Be5 {is also strong, but it is nicer to really bring all the pieces into play.}) 15... Be6
16. Re1 {All of White's pieces participate.} Nbd7 {Final question: what is the best way to capture?} 17. Nxe6 (17. Bxe6 fxe6 18. Rxe6 Qh5 {Spiel geht weiter
}) 17... fxe6 18. Rxe6 $1 {Attack on the queen and threat of a discovered check. Black resigned here.} (18. Bxe6+ {is only a check.} Kh8) 18... Qf7 (18... Qf8 19.
Rxf6+) (18... Qh5 19. Re8# (19. Re5+) (19. Rxf6+)) 19. Re7 $1 (19. Re8+ {wins the queen, but why sacrifice the rook unnecessarily?} Rxe8 20. Bxf7+ Kxf7) (19.
Rxf6 {wins the queen, but unnecessarily sacrifices an exchange. Of course it is still winning.} Nxf6 20. Bxf7+ Kxf7) 19... Nd5 (19... Qxc4 20. Qxc4+) 20.
Rxf7 Kxf7 21. Nxd5 cxd5 22. Bxd5+ {and mate soon.} 1-0

