1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 (3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bc4 {This is not a game by Morphy, but another example for the presentation.} Nxe4 5. Bxf7+ $2 Kxf7 6. Nxe4 {Black now has an uncastled king, but the center and development are more important.} d5 7. Neg5+ Kg8 8. d3 (8. O-O e4 $19) 8... h6 9. Nh3 Bxh3 {Not necessary, but the simplest.} 10. gxh3 Qf6 {Black can now castle artificially and will then stand better.} 11. Be3 Bd6 12. Qe2 Kh7 13. O-O-O Rhf8 14.
Ne1 d4 15. Bd2 Qxf2 $17) 3... exd4 4. Bc4 {Playing a gambit is perfectly acceptable according to chess principles.} Bc5 5. Ng5 $6 {Moving the same piece a second time is less so.} Nh6 $1 {develops a piece.} 6. Nxf7 Nxf7 7. Bxf7+ Kxf7 8. Qh5+ g6 9.
Qxc5 {White has regained the material, but Black has a large lead in development.} d6 10. Qb5 (10. Qc4+ Be6) 10... Re8 11. Qb3+ $2 {moves the queen again.} (11. O-O {The pawn really is hanging.} Rxe4 12. Qd5+ Re6)
(11. f3 $6 Qh4+ $1 12. g3 Rxe4+ $3 (12... Qh3 {Preventing castling is also enough for an advantage.}) 13. Kd1 (13. Kf2 Qf6) 13... Qf6 14. fxe4 Qf3+) (
11. Nd2 d5 $1 $15) 11... d5 {not bad; Kg7 would also be okay here.} 12. f3
{White should have given up the pawn and instead brought the king to safety.} Na5 {The second-best move. It makes sense, however, because it prepares dxe4.} (12... Qh4+ {would have been stronger and gives more attacking chances.} 13. g3 Qh3 {White cannot castle. The d5-pawn can be sacrificed in the process.} 14. Qxd5+ Be6 15. Qb5 Qg2
16. Rf1 Qxc2 $19) 13. Qd3 {White also had other moves, but we want to think from Black's perspective after the move that was played.} dxe4 $1 14. fxe4 Qh4+ 15. g3 Rxe4+
(15... Qxe4+ {would not be worse. It exchanges queens, but in return Black has an extra pawn and active pieces for the endgame.}) 16. Kf2 Qe7 17. Nd2 (17. Bg5
$1 $13 {Both players miss this here. But it belongs to counterplay/active moves.} Qxg5 (17... Qe6 18. Nd2 Rg4 19. Rhe1 {Now White attacks.}) 18.
Qxe4) 17... Re3 {An active move and an attack.} 18. Qb5 c6 {simply gives up the knight to deflect the queen.} 19. Qf1 (19. Qxa5 Re2+ 20. Kf3 (20. Kf1
Bh3+ 21. Kg1 Qe3#) 20... Qe3#) 19... Bh3 $1 {deflects the queen from e2 once again.} 20.
Qd1 (20. Qxh3 Re2+ 21. Kf3 (21. Kg1 Qe3+ 22. Kf1 Qf2#) (21. Kf1 Re1+ 22. Kg2
Qe2#) 21... Qe3+ 22. Kg4 h5+ 23. Kh4 Qe7#) 20... Rf8 {brings the last piece into the attack.} 21. Nf3 Ke8 {White resigned here.} 22. Bxe3 (22. Qxd4 Rfxf3+ 23.
Kg1 Rf1#) 22... Qxe3# 0-1

