{In this game, Morphy faced a strong opponent and had to fight deep into the middlegame.} 1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. d4 exd4 4. Bc4 d6 5. c3 $5 {
Morphy will ein Gambit spielen.} (5. Nxd4 {This is how modern masters would continue.}) 5... dxc3 6. Qb3 $1 {stronger than merely recapturing the pawn.} (6. Ng5 $2 Nh6)
(6. Qd5 $2 Be6) (6. e5 $2 dxe5 7. Bxf7+ Kxf7 8. Nxe5+ Ke8) (6. Nxc3 {Without a concrete plan, White is simply a pawn down.} Nf6 7. O-O Be7 8. Bf4 O-O) 6...
Qe7 7. O-O (7. Ng5 {still achieves nothing.} Nh6) 7... b6 {This finally allows Black to develop the bishop on c8.} (7... cxb2 8. Bxb2 {gives White another developing tempo.}) 8. Nxc3 (8. Bd5 {Do not move a piece twice in the opening without a reason.} Bb7 9. Nxc3 O-O-O) (8. Bf4 cxb2 {White no longer gains a developing tempo.}) 8... Na5 $1 {Black plays strongly and exchanges the powerful bishop. Question: where should the queen move?} 9. Qb4 {Of all queen moves, this one does not lose White a tempo.} (9. Bg5 $2 Nxb3 (9... f6 {[%cal Rf6g5,Ra5b3]}) 10. Bxe7
Nxa1) (9. Qa4+ Bd7 10. Nb5 $2 a6 (10... Qxe4 11. Re1) 11. Nxc7+ Kd8) (9. Qb5+
Bd7) 9... Nxc4 (9... c5 {does force the queen to a4, but c5 is a weakening move.} 10. Qa4+ Bd7 11. Bb5 a6 (11... Bxb5 12. Nxb5 Qd7 (12... Kd8 13. Rd1
Qd7 14. Bf4 a6) 13. Nc7+ Kd8 14. Qxd7+ Kxd7 15. Nxa8) 12. Bxd7+ Qxd7 13. Qc2
$36) 10. Qxc4 Bb7 {We are now in the middlegame. White definitely has compensation for the pawn, but how should White continue?} 11. Re1 {Bring pieces into play.} (11. Ng5 $2 {again moves a piece twice without a reason.} h6
) (11. Nd5 Bxd5 12. exd5 Qd7 {is still solid for Black.}) 11... O-O-O (
11... Nf6 $2 {How should White continue here?} 12. Nd5 $1 {[%cal Rd5e7,Rd5c7]} (12. e5
$2 {also looks strong, but Black can defend against it.} d5 $5 (
12... Bxf3 13. gxf3 {[%cal Rc4c6]} (13. exf6 Qxe1+) 13... O-O-O) 13. exf6 Qxe1+
14. Nxe1 dxc4) 12... Bxd5 13. exd5 $18 {[%cal Re1e7]}) (11... c6 12. Bg5 f6 13.
Bf4 Qd7 14. Rad1 {White has enormous compensation for the pawn, but the game still continues.}) 12. Bf4 {brings another piece into play.} (12. Nb5 {threatens nothing.} Kb8) (12. Nd5 {Black does not have to take the knight here.} Qd7) 12...
f6 {Black prevents e5, but once again hinders Black's own development.} 13. Rac1 {brings the last piece into play.} (13. Nd5 Qd7) (13. a4 $5 {would be another typical modern idea: bringing the rook into the attack.}) 13... Kb8 {Now that all pieces are in play, they gradually have to be moved toward the king.} 14.
Nd5 {Black has to capture this knight.} Bxd5 15. exd5 $1 {activates the rook on e1 and controls the c6-square.} Qd7 16. Nd4 {Move the piece toward the king.} Ne7 17. Rxe7 $3 {Our knight on d4 is more important for the attack than the rook on e1.} (17. Ne6 Rc8 {Black can live with this. For example:} 18. Rc3 c6 19. dxc6 Rxc6 $11) (17. Nc6+ Nxc6 18. dxc6 (18. Qxc6
Qxc6 19. Rxc6 {White has an endgame a pawn down. That is exactly what White does not want.}) 18... Qc8 {It will be difficult to win.}) (17. Qa6 {This should be the first idea, with threats of Nb5 or Nc6+, but Black is in time to defend.} Qc8) 17... Bxe7 18. Qa6 $1 {wichtiger
Zugreihenfolge} (18. Nc6+ {[%cal Gb8c8,Gb8a8,Yb8b7]} Kb7 {Now Qa6 does not work.} (18... Ka8 19. Qa6)) 18... c5 {the only move.} (18... Qc8 19. Nc6+ Ka8 20.
Qxa7# {This is the point of White's exchange sacrifice.}) 19. dxc6 {
[%cal Rc6c7]} Qc8 (19... Qc7 20. Nb5) 20. c7+ Ka8 21. cxd8=Q Qxd8 {There are now two winning options.} 22. Nb5 (22. Nc6 Qd7 (22... Qc7 23. Nxe7 Qxc1+ {The rook is protected.} 24. Bxc1) 23. Nxe7 Qxe7 24. Rc8+ Rxc8 25. Qxc8#) 22... Qb8 23. Rc7 {Black resigned here.} 1-0

